[
 {
  "id": "M19/p1",
  "lesson": "M19",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>How many evaluations of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span> do central differences need for the full gradient?</p>",
  "answer": "2000000",
  "answerKind": "numeric",
  "tol": 1,
  "options": [],
  "explain": "<p>Two million: two per parameter. At a millisecond each, half an hour for one gradient, and training needs millions of gradients. Finite differences are a way to test gradients, not to compute them.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "M19/p2",
  "lesson": "M19",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>Reverse-mode automatic differentiation, which every framework uses, computes the same gradient exactly. About how much does it cost?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>A million times the cost of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>, like forward differences.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>About a thousand times the cost of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>, the square root.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>A small constant times the cost of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>, typically 2 to 4, whatever the number of parameters.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Nothing extra: gradients come for free.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>A small constant times one evaluation. Reverse mode runs the computation forward once, storing intermediate values, then sweeps backward once, at each operation multiplying an incoming row vector by that operation's local derivatives. One sweep produces all million partial derivatives. The price is memory: the stored intermediates.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "M19/d1/s2",
  "lesson": "M19",
  "kind": "derivation",
  "prompt": "<p class=\"review-context\">Shapes of matmul gradients</p><p>Computing it costs <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2Bnm</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">nm</span></span></span></span> operations, the same as the forward product. The backward pass of a matmul layer computes two such products, so its cost is about <span class=\"blank\" data-blank=\"0\">[ 1 ]</span> times the forward pass.</p>",
  "answer": "2",
  "answerKind": "expr"
 },
 {
  "id": "M19/d2/s2",
  "lesson": "M19",
  "kind": "derivation",
