  "prompt": "Why does reverse mode compute a full gradient of a scalar function for about the cost of one evaluation, while forward mode needs one pass per input?",
  "answer": "The gradient is a product of Jacobians, u^T J_K ... J_1 with u = 1 for a scalar output. Starting from the output, every partial product is a single row vector, so each step is a cheap vector-Jacobian product and one backward sweep gives all n partial derivatives. Forward mode multiplies from the input side with one direction at a time, giving one column (one partial derivative) per pass.",
